//? Title: Classical statistical tests by design
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

This report exercises every entry in the statistical test plan. Each table names
the native C++ entry point and records its statistic and p-value. The examples
are small synthetic fixtures. They illustrate the choice of test and calibration,
so compare p-values only within the same test and null hypothesis.

\toc

> Means, paired differences and regression breaks

Use pooled Student only when the groups share a population variance. Welch
estimates separate variances. The two z tests instead take the population
standard deviations as inputs. A paired difference test starts with one
difference per matched row. The table shows both the paired t procedure and the
distribution-free sign alternative. The independent-sample F test compares
variances under normality. The Chow test compares separate coefficient vectors
with a pooled fit, assuming a common residual variance and a prespecified split.

```{cpp, id=mean-tests, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/additional_tests.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/nonparametric.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/t_test.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/variance_test.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <string_view>
#include <vector>

namespace s = datamunge::stats;

int main() {
    const std::vector<double> x{3, 4, 6, 7, 8}, y{2, 5, 4, 5, 6};
    const auto row = [](std::string_view test, std::string_view api, const s::HypothesisTestResult& result) {
        std::println("| {} | `{}` | {:.7g} | {:.7g} |", test, api, result.statistic, result.p_value);
    };
    std::println("| Test | Native entry point | Statistic | p-value |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |");
    row("One-sample t", "t_test_one_sample", s::t_test_one_sample(x, 4));
    row("Paired difference (sign)", "paired_sign_test", s::paired_sign_test(x, y));
    row("Unpaired pooled t", "t_test_two_sample(x, y, true)", s::t_test_two_sample(x, y, true));
    row("Welch t", "t_test_two_sample", s::t_test_two_sample(x, y));
    row("Paired t", "t_test_paired", s::t_test_paired(x, y));
    row("Independent variance F", "f_test_variance", s::f_test_variance(x, y));
    row("One-mean z", "z_test_one_sample", s::z_test_one_sample(x, 2.0, 4));
    row("Two-mean z", "z_test_two_sample", s::z_test_two_sample(x, y, 2.0, 1.5));
    const datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> design(4, 2, {1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 3});
    row("Chow", "chow_test", s::chow_test(design, {0, 1, 3, 3}, design, {1, 3, 6, 7}));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Native entry point | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| One-sample t | `t_test_one_sample` | 1.725324 | 0.1595529 |
| Paired difference (sign) | `paired_sign_test` | 4 | 0.375 |
| Unpaired pooled t | `t_test_two_sample(x, y, true)` | 1.044466 | 0.3268003 |
| Welch t | `t_test_two_sample` | 1.044466 | 0.3294993 |
| Paired t | `t_test_paired` | 2.057983 | 0.108701 |
| Independent variance F | `f_test_variance` | 1.869565 | 0.5593678 |
| One-mean z | `z_test_one_sample` | 1.788854 | 0.07363827 |
| Two-mean z | `z_test_two_sample` | 1.073313 | 0.2831309 |
| Chow | `chow_test` | 21.42857 | 0.007287329 |
// result_end

> Relabeling, ranks and signs

For small independent groups, the exhaustive permutation and exact
Kruskal–Wallis variants enumerate allocations of observation identities,
preserving group sizes. The former uses the between-group sum of squares.
The latter ranks the pooled observations with midranks for ties. For larger
groups there are separate Monte Carlo permutation and asymptotic Kruskal–Wallis
APIs. Friedman ranks treatments within a matched block. Mann–Whitney uses pooled
ranks, Wilcoxon signed-rank uses signed ranks of differences, and Siegel–Tukey
orders observations from the pooled extremes inward to test scale. The sign
test uses only the direction of each nonzero difference.

```{cpp, id=rank-tests, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/additional_tests.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/classical_tests.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/nonparametric.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <string_view>
#include <vector>

namespace s = datamunge::stats;

int main() {
    const std::vector<double> x{1, 2, 3}, y{5, 6, 7};
    const auto row = [](std::string_view test, std::string_view api, const s::HypothesisTestResult& result) {
        std::println("| {} | `{}` | {:.7g} | {:.7g} |", test, api, result.statistic, result.p_value);
    };
    std::println("| Test | Native entry point | Statistic | p-value |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |");
    row("Permutation", "permutation_test_exact", s::permutation_test_exact({x, y, {4, 8}}));
    row("Kruskal–Wallis H", "kruskal_wallis_test_exact", s::kruskal_wallis_test_exact({x, y, {4, 8}}));
    row("Mann–Whitney U", "mann_whitney_test", s::mann_whitney_test(x, y));
    row("Wilcoxon signed-rank", "wilcoxon_signed_rank_exact_test", s::wilcoxon_signed_rank_exact_test({1, 3, 5, 8}));
    row("Sign", "sign_test", s::sign_test({1, 3, 5, 8}));
    row("Friedman", "friedman_test", s::friedman_test({{1, 4, 5}, {3, 2, 4}, {2, 5, 7}, {4, 3, 6}}));
    row("Mood median", "median_test", s::median_test({x, y, {4, 8}}));
    row("Siegel–Tukey", "siegel_tukey_test", s::siegel_tukey_test({0, 1, 9, 10}, {3, 4, 6, 7}));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Native entry point | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| Permutation | `permutation_test_exact` | 30 | 0.075 |
| Kruskal–Wallis H | `kruskal_wallis_test_exact` | 5 | 0.075 |
| Mann–Whitney U | `mann_whitney_test` | 0 | 0.0808556 |
| Wilcoxon signed-rank | `wilcoxon_signed_rank_exact_test` | 10 | 0.125 |
| Sign | `sign_test` | 4 | 0.125 |
| Friedman | `friedman_test` | 6 | 0.04978707 |
| Mood median | `median_test` | 6 | 0.04978707 |
| Siegel–Tukey | `siegel_tukey_test` | 10 | 0.02092134 |
// result_end

> Counts, matched outcomes and contingency tables

Pearson's chi-square statistic compares observed and expected counts in
goodness-of-fit and independence problems. Both use asymptotic references.
For matched binary observations, McNemar conditions on discordant pairs and
Cochran's Q extends the comparison to multiple treatments in each block.
Binomial and multinomial goodness-of-fit use exact discrete null probabilities.

Fisher conditions on both margins. The rectangular version extends the
two-by-two test to a bounded exhaustive enumeration. Barnard and Boschloo
fix only the two sample sizes and maximize their rejection probability over
the common success probability. Barnard orders tables by a pooled score.
Boschloo orders by a one-sided Fisher probability, with twice the smaller
unconditional tail for the two-sided test. The rectangular Fisher,
multinomial and group enumeration APIs have work limits for large supports.

```{cpp, id=count-tests, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/additional_tests.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/chi_squared_test.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/classical_tests.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/fisher_exact_test.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/proportion_test.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <string_view>

namespace s = datamunge::stats;

int main() {
    const auto row = [](std::string_view test, std::string_view api, const s::HypothesisTestResult& result) {
        std::println("| {} | `{}` | {:.7g} | {:.7g} |", test, api, result.statistic, result.p_value);
    };
    std::println("| Test | Native entry point | Statistic | p-value |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |");
    row("Chi-square independence", "chi_squared_test_independence", s::chi_squared_test_independence({18, 32, 30, 20}, 2, 2, false));
    row("Pearson chi-square goodness-of-fit", "chi_squared_goodness_of_fit", s::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit({20, 28, 12}));
    row("Multinomial", "multinomial_test", s::multinomial_test({4, 2, 0}, {.5, .3, .2}));
    row("McNemar", "mcnemar_test", s::mcnemar_test(12, 3));
    row("Cochran Q", "cochran_q_test", s::cochran_q_test({{0, 1, 1}, {0, 0, 1}, {1, 1, 1}, {0, 1, 0}}));
    row("Binomial", "binomial_test", s::binomial_test(8, 10));
    row("Fisher 2×2", "fisher_exact_test_2x2", s::fisher_exact_test_2x2(7, 12, 8, 3));
    row("Fisher rectangular", "fisher_exact_test", s::fisher_exact_test({{1, 3, 2}, {4, 0, 2}}));
    row("Barnard", "barnard_exact_test", s::barnard_exact_test(7, 12, 8, 3));
    row("Boschloo", "boschloo_exact_test", s::boschloo_exact_test(7, 12, 8, 3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Native entry point | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| Chi-square independence | `chi_squared_test_independence` | 5.769231 | 0.01630917 |
| Pearson chi-square goodness-of-fit | `chi_squared_goodness_of_fit` | 6.4 | 0.0407622 |
| Multinomial | `multinomial_test` | 0.084375 | 0.6625 |
| McNemar | `mcnemar_test` | 12 | 0.03515625 |
| Cochran Q | `cochran_q_test` | 2.666667 | 0.2635971 |
